#
# Network-detection hook for intel-mips.
#
# The front-panel jacks are DSA user ports. Their names come from the
# "label" property of each port node in the device tree, so they are stable
# across probe order and across boards, and the software bridge netifd
# builds over them is a bridge over switch ports.

. /lib/functions.sh
. /lib/functions/uci-defaults.sh
. /lib/functions/system.sh

# fritz_tffs_mac(), used by both arms below to read the per-unit Ethernet
# addresses. It sits in its own file because the 7580's WiFi hotplug script
# needs the same reader; the invocation details are documented there.
. /lib/functions/fritz.sh

board_config_update

case "$(board_name)" in
avm,fritz7560)
	#
	# All four front-panel jacks ship as LAN. This board has no WAN
	# Ethernet socket — AVM's uplink here was DSL, which is out of
	# scope for this port — so there is nothing to nominate as WAN in
	# the hardware description. This matches the lantiq/xrx200
	# AVM siblings (avm,fritz7360sl, avm,fritz7362sl, avm,fritz7430,
	# avm,fritz7490, avm,fritz3490, ...), all of which declare every
	# jack as LAN; it is the upstream-faithful default for AVM boxes
	# without a WAN jack.
	#
	# To use the box as a router, nominate one LAN port as WAN at
	# runtime. That is plain OpenWrt uci configuration — no image
	# change needed — and the stock firewall already ships the wan
	# zone with masquerade, so the port starts routing as soon as it
	# is moved. LAN1 is the port to recommend, following AVM's own
	# uplink convention.
	#
	# lan1..lan4 are the DSA port labels, not probe-order names. The
	# board wires front-panel LAN-N to GPHY-(6-N) on switch port (6-N),
	# and seale_avm.dtsi carries that reversal in the labels, so lan1 is
	# the socket marked LAN 1 whatever the port numbering says. Nothing
	# here has to know the mapping.
	#
	ucidef_set_interface_lan "lan1 lan2 lan3 lan4"

	#
	# MAC addresses. This unit's addresses live in the AVM TFFS
	# partition, and nothing in the kernel reads them, so without the
	# lines below every port comes up with a fresh random address on each
	# boot. DSA derives a user port's address from the conduit's, and the
	# conduit has no source for one either.
	#
	# The invocation details (char device for MEMREADOOB, -o plus the
	# plain-read fallback, no -b, filtered output) live with
	# fritz_tffs_mac() in /lib/functions/fritz.sh. The partition is looked
	# up by label rather than by index: mtd numbering shifts with the
	# partition table, the label does not.
	tffsdev=$(find_mtd_chardev "nand-tffs")
	maca=$(fritz_tffs_mac "$tffsdev" maca)

	# Anything unexpected out of the tool — an empty string, an error
	# message, a partition that could not be found — leaves the random
	# addresses in place rather than feeding junk into the network
	# config. A broken board.d is worse than boring MACs.
	case "$maca" in
	[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F])
		#
		# All four ports get maca, which is what the box shipped
		# with: AVM's own device tree tags every one of the four
		# LAN nodes with avm,macname = "maca". They are ports of
		# one switch bridged together here, so sharing one address
		# across them is also what the hardware looks like.
		#
		# Per-device, because board.json's "network_device" table is
		# netifd's own default-MAC source: config_get_default_macaddr()
		# consults it for every device it brings up, configured in
		# /etc/config/network or not, and preinit reads it as well.
		# It is a *default*, though, and defaults stop at the bridge:
		# bridge_reset_primary() takes the bridge address from its
		# first member's original one unless that member carries an
		# explicitly configured macaddr. So these four calls dress the
		# ports and failsafe, not br-lan.
		#
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan1 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan2 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan3 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan4 "$maca"

		# ...and per-interface, which is what carries the address into
		# the /etc/config/network that config_generate() writes: it
		# stamps an explicit macaddr on the device section of every
		# port of the bridge it builds for the interface. That is the
		# configured member address bridge_reset_primary() looks for,
		# so br-lan adopts maca as well, whichever port comes up first.
		#
		ucidef_set_interface_macaddr "lan" "$maca"

		ucidef_set_label_macaddr "$maca"
		;;
	esac

	#
	# WiFi. The country is not cosmetic here: wifi-scripts' mac80211.uc
	# reads wlan.defaults.country and, when it is empty, nulls the
	# defaults block for every band except 2g — which comes out as
	# disabled='1' on the resulting 5 GHz wifi-iface. So leaving this out
	# brings the 5 GHz radio up and then switches it off. DE matches
	# where these boxes were sold.
	#
	ucidef_set_country "DE"

	#
	# Naming the two radios by device path pins which one is wl0 and
	# which is wl1 rather than leaving it to PCI probe order: mac80211.sh
	# renames each phy to the board.json key whose path it matches. The
	# order of these two calls is what assigns the index, so 5 GHz first.
	#
	# The paths are the sysfs device paths with the leading "platform/"
	# stripped, which is the shape wifi-detect.uc's phy_path() produces
	# for PCI-attached phys (note ucidef_set_network_device_path keeps
	# that prefix — only the wlan one drops it). The domain numbers are
	# not probe-order artefacts: xrx500.dtsi pins them with
	# linux,pci-domain, 0 for the root complex at 0x18900000 and 1 for the
	# one at 0x18400000. The 01:00.0 follows from each bridge having a
	# single root port at 00:00.0.
	#
	# A path that matches no live phy is harmless — wifi-detect.uc leaves
	# the entry without .info, mac80211.uc skips it, and the phy picks up
	# an auto-generated entry as it would have anyway.
	#
	ucidef_add_wlan 18900000.pcie/pci0000:00/0000:00:00.0/0000:01:00.0
	ucidef_add_wlan 18400000.pcie/pci0001:00/0001:00:00.0/0001:01:00.0
	;;
avm,fritz7580|avm,fritz7590)
	#
	# Unlike the 7560, these boards have a dedicated WAN jack, so they
	# can declare a real lan/wan split rather than shipping every socket
	# as LAN and asking the user to nominate an uplink at runtime. Both
	# wire it the same way, which is why one arm serves them: it is the
	# GRX550 die's fifth port, and everything below follows from that
	# rather than from either chassis.
	#
	# The names are DSA port labels out of the device tree, so they are
	# the same five names on both boards and on every boot.
	#
	# The LAN four are chassis order left to right, because the dtsi
	# already undoes the board's reversed wiring: front-panel LAN-N goes
	# to GPHY-(6-N) on the LAN macro's port (6-N) — LAN1 to phy@5, LAN4
	# to phy@2 — and each port node carries the label of the socket it
	# actually reaches.
	#
	# wan is the WAN jack, and it is a port of the second switch macro
	# rather than the first. That macro is a switch tree of its own with
	# its own conduit, and wan is the only port whose PHY answers on its
	# management bus.
	#
	ucidef_set_interfaces_lan_wan "lan1 lan2 lan3 lan4" "wan"

	#
	# MAC addresses, read out of the AVM TFFS partition through
	# fritz_tffs_mac() — see its comment, and the 7560 arm for the
	# label-lookup rationale.
	#
	# These boards need two keys rather than one. AVM tags all four LAN
	# nodes avm,macname = "maca" and the WAN node avm,macname = "macdsl",
	# so the WAN port carries an address of its own. The distance between
	# the two keys is a per-unit fact and not a rule to derive one from
	# the other, which is why both are read out of TFFS. Giving the WAN
	# port its own address is not cosmetic either: it is the address the
	# ISP has seen from that unit.
	#
	tffsdev=$(find_mtd_chardev "nand-tffs")
	maca=$(fritz_tffs_mac "$tffsdev" maca)
	macdsl=$(fritz_tffs_mac "$tffsdev" macdsl)

	# Each key is validated on its own, so a unit missing one of them
	# still gets the other rather than falling back to random addresses
	# on both.
	case "$maca" in
	[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F])
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan1 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan2 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan3 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac lan4 "$maca"
		ucidef_set_interface_macaddr "lan" "$maca"
		ucidef_set_label_macaddr "$maca"
		;;
	esac

	case "$macdsl" in
	[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F])
		ucidef_set_network_device_mac wan "$macdsl"
		ucidef_set_interface_macaddr "wan" "$macdsl"
		;;
	esac

	#
	# Same reasoning as the 7560: an empty country nulls the defaults
	# block for every band above 2.4 GHz, which brings the 5 GHz radio up
	# and then disables it.
	#
	ucidef_set_country "DE"

	#
	# Both radios are QCA9984 on both boards, so unlike the 7560 — where
	# the two parts are different silicon — the order of these calls is
	# the only thing deciding which band gets which index. Domain 0
	# first, which keeps wl0 the 5 GHz radio across all three devices.
	# Which blob belongs to which domain is settled in 11-ath10k-caldata.
	#
	# Which band sits on which root complex is a board fact rather than a
	# family convention. AVM's own device tree settles it for both boards:
	# the 5 GHz part sits on the root complex at 0x18900000 and the
	# 2.4 GHz one at 0x18400000, which xrx500.dtsi pins to domains 0 and
	# 1, so both boards want these two lines in the order they already
	# stand in.
	#
	# Paths as on the 7560: the sysfs device path with the leading
	# "platform/" stripped, and domain numbers that come from
	# linux,pci-domain in xrx500.dtsi rather than from probe order.
	#
	ucidef_add_wlan 18900000.pcie/pci0000:00/0000:00:00.0/0000:01:00.0
	ucidef_add_wlan 18400000.pcie/pci0001:00/0001:00:00.0/0001:01:00.0
	;;
esac

board_config_flush

exit 0
